      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Human studies have demonstrated that allergen immunotherapy induces memory suppressive responses and IL-10 production by allergen-specific T cells. Previously, we established a mouse model in which allergen immunotherapy was effective in the suppression of allergen-induced asthma manifestations."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "In this study, we examined whether immunotherapy induces a long-lasting effect and investigated the role of IL-10 in successful immunotherapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Ovalbumin-sensitized BALB/c mice were treated with 3 injections of ovalbumin (1 mg, subcutaneous) on alternate days. After a short interval (1 week) and after a long interval (5 weeks), mice were challenged by ovalbumin inhalation, and subsequently, airway reactivity, airway eosinophilia, ovalbumin-specific IgE, and T(H)2 cytokine profile were measured. Flow cytometry and blocking of IL-10 receptors in vivo were used to gain insight in the role of IL-10 in the beneficial effects of allergen immunotherapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "After a long interval between ovalbumin immunotherapy and ovalbumin challenge, the development of airway eosinophilia and hyperresponsiveness to methacholine were as strongly suppressed as after a short interval. These suppressive effects coincided with significantly reduced serum ovalbumin-specific IgE levels and T(H)2 cytokine production. On immunotherapy, the IL-5:IL-10 ratio in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid shifted toward IL-10. In ovalbumin-restimulated lung cell and thoracic lymph node cultures from these mice, IL-5 levels dramatically decreased, whereas the percentage of IL-10(+)CD4(+) T cells was not affected. Finally, in mice treated with mAb against IL-10 receptors, the beneficial effects of immunotherapy were largely abrogated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "These data demonstrate that allergen immunotherapy induces a memory suppressive effect in which IL-10 is essential."
        }
      ]},
